### What changes were proposed in this pull request?

Under whole-stage codegen, `CodegenContext.splitExpressionsWithCurrentInputs` did not split at all (`// TODO: support whole stage codegen`), so the code of every branch of a `CASE WHEN` went into the operator's method. Outside a stage the same code is split into methods that take the input row; inside a stage the inputs are local variables of the operator's method, and which of them a piece of code reads is not known from the code strings the method receives. This PR teaches it to split under whole-stage codegen when the caller also passes the expressions the code was generated for, and makes `CaseWhen` pass them.

From those expressions, a new private method, `CodegenContext.wholeStageSplitArguments`, computes the arguments of the split methods: the local variables the code reads from the operator's method. It walks the expressions the way the existing split mechanisms under whole-stage codegen do. It takes the evaluated input variables of `currentVars`, and `INPUT_ROW` only where the code reads it (an input not in `currentVars`, or a `CodegenFallback`), since an operator may leave it naming a local that is out of scope there, as a join does for its build side; as `CodeGenerator.getLocalInputVariableValues` does for aggregates, subexpressions and `ExpandExec`. It takes the variables of a subexpression elimination state without descending below it, as that method does too, so that the split methods' arguments are among those of an enclosing method an aggregate or `ExpandExec` has split out, where the calls then sit. And it follows a `CommonExpressionRef` into its definition, as `CommonExprSlots.methodArgs` does for `With`. Fields and literals need no argument.

It refuses, and the code stays in one piece as before, where a local the code reads cannot be named or passed:
- an input the operator has not evaluated yet (a projection defers reading a column it uses once): its code is inside this code and reads locals of the operator producing the row, as `methodArgs` explains;
- a value no parameter can carry: a `SimpleExprValue` (a slot of a compacted state array is a field and needs no argument);
- a subexpression state for a node that overrides `genCode` (`Alias`, `Collate`, an identity `Cast` today), which may generate its child instead of reading the state;
- a `LambdaVariable`, whose value is a local of an object expression's loop;
- more parameter slots than a method can take, counting the caller's `extraArguments`.

The walk runs only when the code would be split into more than one block.

Callers that do not pass the expressions keep today's behavior. The split is on by default and can be turned off with the internal `spark.sql.codegen.wholeStage.splitExpressions`, as `spark.sql.codegen.aggregate.splitAggregateFunc.enabled` can for the aggregate split.

### Why are the changes needed?

SPARK-33301: a large `CASE WHEN` fails to compile inside a stage. On Spark 4.2.0, one `CASE WHEN` of `n` branches over a long column,

```scala
val branches = (1 to n).map(k => s"WHEN id = $k THEN id * $k").mkString(" ")
spark.sql(s"SELECT CASE $branches ELSE 0 END AS v FROM range(10)").collect()
```

compiles at 952 branches and fails at 953 ("Code grows beyond 64 KB"). Spark logs "Failed to compile the generated Java code" and "Whole-stage codegen disabled for plan" and runs the stage without whole-stage codegen. Earlier, from 138 branches, the stage's method is past 8000 bytes and HotSpot never JIT-compiles it (`-XX:+DontCompileHugeMethods`), so the stage runs interpreted. With this change the branches are split into methods at both sizes.

### Does this PR introduce _any_ user-facing change?

No, other than the performance of such queries. The answers are the same; a large `CASE WHEN` inside a stage now compiles and is JIT-compiled.

The split starts at `spark.sql.codegen.methodSplitThreshold` (1024 characters of source), the same threshold the path without whole-stage codegen uses, so it also splits `CASE WHEN`s whose unsplit stage method is still under HotSpot's 8000-byte limit. There it can be slower: `CaseWhenCodegenBenchmark` at 64 branches (6049 bytes unsplit) is 13-21% slower with the split on JDK 17, 21 and 25, because C2 compiles the unsplit method as one unit but stops inlining partway through the chain of split calls. Up to 32 branches the two are within the noise, and from 128 branches, where the unsplit method is past 8000 bytes and never JIT-compiled, the split is 14-26x faster. Raising `spark.sql.codegen.methodSplitThreshold` keeps such `CASE WHEN`s in one piece.

### How was this patch tested?

New tests in `WholeStageCodegenSuite`:
- a 3000-branch `CASE WHEN` compiles inside a stage and answers (before: the compile error, thrown under testing);
- at 300 branches the stage's methods stay under 8000 bytes, and with the config off the method is past 8000 bytes;
- the split methods take nullable and string columns and subexpression locals (`upper(s)` in every condition);
- they take the aggregation buffer row of a keyed aggregate, called from inside the aggregate's own split method;
- a `CASE WHEN` that is itself a split common subexpression;
- a `CASE WHEN` reading a column the projection has not evaluated stays in one piece;
- a large `CASE WHEN` in sort-merge and broadcast join conditions, and in the streamed-only condition of a left outer join, compiles;
- a `CASE WHEN` above a `ROLLUP` reads the compacted mutable state `ExpandExec` writes as fields and is split.

Existing suites: `CodeGenerationSuite`, `ConditionalExpressionSuite`, `SubexpressionEliminationSuite`, `CodegenSubexpressionEliminationSuite` and `WithExpressionEvalSuite` in catalyst; `WholeStageCodegenSuite`, `TPCDSQuerySuite`, `TPCDSQueryWithStatsSuite`, `TPCDSQueryANSISuite`, `TPCHQuerySuite` and `DataFrameAggregateSuite` in sql. No TPC-DS stage generates code differently: none has a multi-branch `CASE WHEN` inside a stage (a single-branch one is generated as `If`).

Beyond the unit tests, with a temporary change (not in this PR) that splits every `CASE WHEN` inside a stage into one method per branch, so that the argument computation is exercised on every such expression:
- `SQLQueryTestSuite`, `SQLQuerySuite`, the DataFrame and Dataset suites, `DataFrameAggregateSuite`, `JoinSuite` and `WholeStageCodegenSuite` pass (3162 tests; 240 `CASE WHEN`s split, 11 refused), apart from `udtf/udtf.sql`, whose Python error message differs by Python version here;
- a differential fuzz of 600 random queries over a Parquet table (nullable numeric, string, date, decimal, array, struct and boolean columns) and nine operator shapes -- a projection, a repeated `CASE WHEN` (a split common subexpression), keyed and unkeyed aggregates, a filter, broadcast and sort-merge joins, `explode`, `ROLLUP`, a `CASE WHEN` inside a lambda, `ORDER BY ... LIMIT` -- with nested `CASE WHEN`s in conditions and values, compared against the same queries without whole-stage codegen: no difference, no compile error, 2343 splits and 430 refusals (all of them an input the operator had not evaluated). 300 more at the default threshold: no difference.

This PR builds on SPARK-59783 (#59042, merged), which groups the calls to split functions: without it, at 1000 branches the method holding the calls to the split methods is itself past 8000 bytes and runs interpreted. With it, on a laptop, the 1000-branch query over 2M rows takes about 0.40 s a run in a split stage against 1.70 s without whole-stage codegen (measured after the review fixes); at 300 branches the split stage is 2.8x faster than without whole-stage codegen and 22x faster than today's unsplit stage, whose method HotSpot never compiles. A `CASE WHEN` inside a common subexpression is generated twice when subexpression elimination moves to its split pass; the methods split out of the discarded first generation are removed from the class.

