issues.body:
## Title

[v2 Bug] Source freshness reports a bogus ~56-year staleness (silently falls back to Unix epoch) when `MAX(loaded_at_field)` is NULL on an empty table

## Description

When a source's `loaded_at_field` query (`MAX(loaded_at_field)`) returns SQL `NULL` — which happens whenever the underlying table has zero rows — `dbt freshness` / `dbt source freshness` does not report an error or a "no data" state. Instead it silently treats the missing timestamp as the Unix epoch (`1970-01-01T00:00:00Z`) and reports a nonsensical staleness of several decades.

### Steps to reproduce (real project)

```yaml
# models/staging/my_source.yml
sources:
  - name: my_source
    tables:
      - name: empty_table   # currently has 0 rows
        config:
          freshness:
            warn_after: {count: 7, period: day}
            error_after: {count: 14, period: day}
          loaded_at_field: extracted_at
```

```
$ dbt freshness --select source:my_source.empty_table
     Stale source my_source.empty_table (last updated 56years 8months 26days 8h 2s ago) [2.45s]
[error] [StaleSource (dbt1063)]: Stale source source.my_source.empty_table
```

`SELECT MAX(extracted_at), COUNT(*) FROM empty_table` on the actual table returns `(NULL, 0)` — there is no data, not 56-year-old data.

### Minimal standalone reproduction (proves the exact mechanism)

I isolated the bug outside of the full dbt binary by replicating the cast/extract pipeline from `calculate_freshness_common` verbatim, using `arrow = "56.0.0"` and `datafusion-common = "50.3.0"` (the public crates.io versions the workspace's `[patch.crates-io]` section pins forks of — I could not access the private `sdf-labs/arrow-rs` / `sdf-labs/datafusion` forks directly, but this null-handling behavior is documented, stable Arrow semantics, not something a fork would plausibly change).

`Cargo.toml`:
```toml
[dependencies]
arrow = { version = "=56.0.0", features = ["prettyprint"] }
datafusion-common = { version = "=50.3.0", default-features = false, features = ["object_store", "parquet"] }
chrono = "0.4"
```

`src/main.rs`:
```rust
use arrow::array::{ArrayRef, RecordBatch, TimestampMicrosecondArray};
use arrow::compute::cast_with_options;
use arrow::datatypes::{DataType, Field, Schema, TimeUnit};
use chrono::Utc;
use datafusion_common::cast::as_timestamp_nanosecond_array;
use datafusion_common::format::DEFAULT_CAST_OPTIONS;
use std::sync::Arc;

fn main() {
    // max_loaded_at: NULL (simulates MAX(extracted_at) over 0 rows)
    let max_loaded_at_array: ArrayRef =
        Arc::new(TimestampMicrosecondArray::from(vec![None]).with_timezone("UTC"));

    // snapshotted_at: "now"
    let now_micros = Utc::now().timestamp_micros();
    let snapshotted_at_array: ArrayRef =
        Arc::new(TimestampMicrosecondArray::from(vec![Some(now_micros)]).with_timezone("UTC"));

    let schema = Arc::new(Schema::new(vec![
        Field::new("max_loaded_at", DataType::Timestamp(TimeUnit::Microsecond, Some("UTC".into())), true),
        Field::new("snapshotted_at", DataType::Timestamp(TimeUnit::Microsecond, Some("UTC".into())), true),
    ]));

    let batch = RecordBatch::try_new(schema, vec![max_loaded_at_array, snapshotted_at_array]).unwrap();

    println!("max_loaded_at is_null(0)  = {}", batch.column(0).is_null(0));

    // ---- verbatim pipeline from freshness.rs calculate_freshness_common ----
    let max_loaded_at = as_timestamp_nanosecond_array(&cast_with_options(
        batch.column(0).as_ref(),
        &DataType::Timestamp(TimeUnit::Nanosecond, Some("UTC".into())),
        &DEFAULT_CAST_OPTIONS,
    ).unwrap()).unwrap().value(0); // <-- unchecked accessor

    let snapshotted_at = as_timestamp_nanosecond_array(&cast_with_options(
        batch.column(1).as_ref(),
        &DataType::Timestamp(TimeUnit::Nanosecond, Some("UTC".into())),
        &DEFAULT_CAST_OPTIONS,
    ).unwrap()).unwrap().value(0);

    let age_seconds = (snapshotted_at - max_loaded_at) / 1_000_000_000;
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------

    println!("max_loaded_at raw value via .value(0) = {}", max_loaded_at);
    println!("age = {} seconds = {:.2} years", age_seconds, age_seconds as f64 / (365.25 * 24.0 * 3600.0));
}
```

Output:
```
max_loaded_at is_null(0)  = true
max_loaded_at raw value via .value(0) = 0
age = 1790544888 seconds = 56.74 years
```

This closely matches the exact staleness reported against the real, empty BigQuery table (~56.9 years; the small difference is only due to the two runs happening at different times).

### Root cause

[`crates/dbt-freshness/src/freshness.rs#L443-L473`](https://github.com/dbt-labs/dbt/blob/56cf8733485e2c97ef1e49682620045fc5726bf8/crates/dbt-freshness/src/freshness.rs#L443-L473) (`calculate_freshness_common`):

```rust
let max_loaded_at_column = validate_and_extract_timestamp_column(
    &batch,
    MAX_LOADED_AT_COLUMN,
    node,
    error_message,
    io_args,
)?;
...
let max_loaded_at = as_timestamp_nanosecond_array(&cast_with_options(
    max_loaded_at_column.as_ref(),
    &DataType::Timestamp(TimeUnit::Nanosecond, Some("UTC".into())),
    &DEFAULT_CAST_OPTIONS,
)?)?
.value(0);
...
let age = (snapshotted_at - max_loaded_at) / 1_000_000_000;
```

[`validate_and_extract_timestamp_column`](https://github.com/dbt-labs/dbt/blob/56cf8733485e2c97ef1e49682620045fc5726bf8/crates/dbt-freshness/src/freshness.rs#L1036-L1068) only validates the column's Arrow **data type** (`Timestamp(_, _)` vs. anything else). Neither this function nor anything else in the file checks whether the value at index 0 is actually null (`grep -c "is_null\|is_valid\|null_count" crates/dbt-freshness/src/freshness.rs` returns `0`).

`arrow::array::PrimitiveArray::value(i)` is documented to ignore the null bitmap and return whatever is in the underlying buffer at that physical index ("Note: This does not check for nulls, the value at a null index may be arbitrary."). For a value converted from SQL `NULL`, that buffer slot is `0`, i.e. the Unix epoch — which is exactly what both the standalone repro and the real `dbt freshness` run against an empty table produce.

### Expected behavior

When `MAX(loaded_at_field)` is `NULL` (table has no rows yet), `dbt freshness` should either:
- report a clear, distinct state (e.g. "no data / cannot determine freshness") instead of computing an age at all, or
- treat it as maximally stale without fabricating a specific, misleading duration.

Either is preferable to silently reporting a specific-looking but meaningless number of years, which reads as a real (if surprising) measurement rather than an error condition — this is misleading in CI/Slack alerting where only the pass/fail and the message are visible, not the raw row count.

### Suggested fix direction

In `calculate_freshness_common`, check `is_null(0)` on the extracted `max_loaded_at_column` (and/or on `snapshotted_at`, defensively) before calling `.value(0)`, and short-circuit with an explicit error (or a dedicated "no data" `FreshnessResult`/status) instead of computing `age` from a garbage value. I have not been able to build the full `dbt` workspace locally to implement/test this against the real crate — the checked-in `[patch.crates-io]` section pins `arrow`/`datafusion*` to the private `sdf-labs/arrow-rs` and `sdf-labs/datafusion` repositories, which I don't have access to (`git clone` / cargo fetch fails with an authentication error). I don't know whether that's expected for external contributors or an oversight in the current `Cargo.toml`.

### Environment

- dbt version: 2.0.6 (Fusion)
- Adapter: BigQuery
- OS: macOS (issue is adapter/engine-agnostic — the bug is in the Arrow/DataFusion glue code, not BigQuery-specific)

---

I'd like to pick this up myself and open a PR for it, once I confirm the fix direction above is the right one (and get some guidance on building/testing locally given the private `[patch.crates-io]` dependencies noted above).

## Describe the bug

`match_version` in `crates/dbt-scheduler/src/node_selector.rs` decides the `version:none` selector by probing the model's file stem for a `_v1` or `_v2` suffix. Every other arm of the same function reads the parsed version, and dbt-core's reference implementation decides it from the parsed version too.

The other three arms:

```rust
"latest" => match (&node.version(), &node.latest_version()) { ... }
```

The `none` arm, before this report:

```rust
let file_name = node
    .common()
    .original_file_path
    .file_name()
    .expect("Model should have a file name");
let file_stem = Path::new(file_name)
    .file_stem()
    .expect("file name should have a stem");
let file_stem = file_stem.to_string_lossy();
let file_version = if file_stem.ends_with("_v1") {
    Some(1)
} else if file_stem.ends_with("_v2") {
    Some(2)
} else {
    None
};
// ...
"none" => Ok(file_version.is_none()),
```

## Why it is wrong

dbt-core's `VersionSelectorMethod` reads the parsed version and never the file name:

```python
elif selector == "none":
    if node.version is None:
        yield unique_id
```

A Fusion version is the raw YAML value stringified. From `collect_model_version_info` in `crates/dbt-parser/src/resolve/resolve_properties.rs`:

```rust
let version = match &v.v {
    dbt_yaml::Value::String(s, _) => Some(s.to_string()),
    dbt_yaml::Value::Number(n, _) => Some(n.to_string()),
    _ => None,
}
.unwrap_or_else(|| {
    panic!("Version '{:?}' does not meet the required format", v.v);
});

let versioned_name = format!("{}_v{}", model.name, version);

let defined_in = v
    .defined_in
    .as_deref()
    .map(|s| s.strip_suffix(".sql").unwrap_or(s).to_string());
```

So the version is not limited to 1 and 2, and `defined_in:` can place it in a file with any name. The model field is `version: Option<StringOrInteger>`, so it can be a string, which no numeric suffix probe can recover. A two-value suffix test misclassifies every model outside `_v1` and `_v2`, and produces a false positive in the other direction as well: dbt only makes `orders_v1` a version when `versions:` declares it, so an unversioned model that merely ends in `_v1` is wrongly excluded from `version:none`.

| project state | correct | current code |
|---|---|---|
| `orders` with `versions: [v:1, v:2, v:3]`, file `orders_v3.sql` | no match | matches |
| `orders` with `v: 1.0`, file `orders_v1.0.sql` | no match | matches |
| `orders` with `versions: [v:1]` and `defined_in: orders_hist.sql` | no match | matches |
| unversioned model named `orders_v1`, no `versions:` | matches | does not match |

A model that a user has not versioned is selected by `version:none` on their behalf, and a model on version 3 or `1.0` is silently left out of a `version:none` selection.

## Additional problem

The probe ran two `expect()` calls on every `match_version` call, including for models whose `original_file_path` has no file name.

## Steps to reproduce

Unit level, no database or network needed. Vaporetto-free: construct a `DbtModel` with a `version` and an `original_file_path` that disagree.

```rust
let mut node = create_test_node("model.test.orders.v3", vec!["col1"]);
Arc::get_mut(&mut node).unwrap().__common_attr__.original_file_path =
    "models/orders_v3.sql".into();
Arc::get_mut(&mut node).unwrap().__model_attr__.version =
    Some(StringOrInteger::from("3".to_string()));
assert!(!match_version("none", node.as_ref()).unwrap());
```

## Environment

- dbt-core `main` at `7e4e937c4eef07e3837960507dea46c15c1db42b`
- Rust 1.98.0, aarch64-apple-darwin

## Fix

Drop the suffix probe and read the parsed version, which is what the other three arms already do:

```rust
"none" => Ok(node.version().is_none()),
```

This also removes the two `expect()` calls. `Path` stays imported and used elsewhere in the file.
